import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>9.3c — Polymorphism: Build a Turn-Based Battle System</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>



<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Expand Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Now pull everything together: a complete turn-based battle system that
        operates entirely on Creature references. No Goblin, Dragon, or Skeleton
        types appear in the battle logic — yet each creature fights with its own
        unique behavior.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>This is the pattern that makes RPG engines extensible.</strong>
        When you add a new creature type six months from now, the battle system
        works without changing a single line.
    </div>

    <h3>Build: BattleSystem.java</h3>

    <p>The system has three parts:</p>

    <p><strong>Part 1: Team Setup</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Player's party: ArrayList&lt;Creature&gt; with 3 creatures (choose from Goblin, Dragon, Skeleton — give them names and unique stats)</li>
        <li>Enemy party: ArrayList&lt;Creature&gt; with 3 creatures (different mix)</li>
        <li>Store both teams in a <code>Battle</code> class that holds the two ArrayLists</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Part 2: The Turn Loop</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Each round: player's alive creatures each attack a random enemy</li>
        <li>Then: each surviving enemy attacks a random player creature</li>
        <li>After each attack: check if the target died (HP <= 0) — if so, remove from team</li>
        <li>Continue until one team has no living creatures</li>
    </ul>

    <pre><code>═══ BATTLE ═══
Round 1
  Snag (Goblin) attacks Pyrax (Dragon) for 8 damage!
  Pyrax has 92 HP remaining.
  Elara (Skeleton) attacks Bones (Skeleton) for 5 damage!
  Bones has 25 HP remaining.
  ...

═══ ROUND SUMMARY ═══
Your team: 2 alive, 1 defeated
Enemy team: 1 alive, 2 defeated

Your team wins!</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Your <code>selectRandomTarget(ArrayList&lt;Creature&gt; enemies)</code> method works with Creature references. How do you ensure you only pick ALIVE targets?</li>
            <li>After a creature dies, should you remove it from the ArrayList immediately or mark it dead and skip it? What happens to the ArrayList indices if you remove during iteration?</li>
            <li>Each creature's <code>attack()</code> method returns different damage amounts (Goblin 3-8, Dragon 15-30, Skeleton 5-12). How does the battle loop handle this without knowing the type?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Special Abilities</h3>
        <p>
            Add a <code>specialAbility()</code> method to Creature. Each subclass
            defines its own:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Goblin:</strong> Steal gold — reduces enemy attack by 2 next turn</li>
            <li><strong>Dragon:</strong> Fire breath — hits ALL enemies for 10-15 damage</li>
            <li><strong>Skeleton:</strong> Reassemble — if HP is 0, 20% chance to revive with 10 HP</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Each creature has a 25% chance to use its special ability each turn instead
            of a normal attack. The battle loop checks <code>Math.random() < 0.25</code>
            and calls <code>specialAbility()</code> — still polymorphically, still without
            knowing the creature type.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Concurrent Modification</h3>
        <p>
            If you remove dead creatures from the ArrayList inside a for-each loop,
            Java throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>. Try it — then
            research two solutions:
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>Use a standard for loop running backward (i--) so removal doesn't shift indices you haven't visited yet</li>
            <li>Use an Iterator with <code>iterator.remove()</code></li>
        </ol>
        <p>Which approach is cleaner? Which one avoids the bug entirely?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Build a complete turn-based system using only parent-type references</li>
            <li>Handle creature death and removal from battle safely</li>
            <li>Add special abilities polymorphically without modifying battle logic</li>
            <li>Debug ConcurrentModificationException and implement safe removal</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/polymorphism.html">Polymorphism</a> — full reference</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/extends-and-super.html">Inheritance</a> — superclass patterns</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/arraylist-basics.html">ArrayList</a> — iteration and concurrent modification</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/while-loops.html">while Loops</a> — game loop patterns</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
